#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:0
msgid ""
"List, start, stop, restart, enable, disable, and inspect snap service logs "
"at user and system scope for individual services or all services in a snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:8
msgid "Control services"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:10
msgid ""
"The majority of snaps expose their functionality via applications that run "
"on the local system. Most of these applications are started either from {ref}"
"`the command line <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-apps-and-aliases>`, the "
"graphical desktop, or as services that run automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:12
msgid ""
"A single snap may provide multiple applications and services. With a "
"database snap, for example, you might expect an interactive client "
"application alongside the service daemon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:14
msgid ""
"Snaps manage their own services without the need for manual intervention. "
"However, experienced administrators may want to interact with a snap's "
"services to help improve their integration with the local environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:16
msgid ""
"For that reason, snapd offers a set of commands to allow a snap's services "
"to be inspected and their statuses changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:18
msgid "User and root-level services"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:20
msgid ""
"Prior to the release of _snapd 2.66_, all services were run with root-level "
"access. The snap commands used to manage these services would require "
"equivalent access, such as by preceding *snap* with `sudo`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:22
msgid ""
"With the release of snapd 2.66, there is now distinction between user-level "
"services and root-level services. User-level services can be listed and "
"managed without requiring any privilege escalation, while services running "
"as root still require equivalent access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:24
msgid "Listing services"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:26
msgid ""
"The `snap services` command lists the active and enabled state of snap-based "
"services for the current user, or with the additional `--global` argument, "
"for the entire system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:29
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap services --global  \n"
"Service                    Startup  Current   Notes\n"
"lxd.activate               enabled  inactive  -\n"
"lxd.daemon                 enabled  active    socket-activated\n"
"lxd.user-daemon            enabled  inactive  socket-activated\n"
"multipass.multipassd       enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.apache           enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.logrotate        enabled  inactive  timer-activated\n"
"nextcloud.mysql            enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.nextcloud-cron   enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.nextcloud-fixer  enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.php-fpm          enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.redis-server     enabled  active    -\n"
"nextcloud.renew-certs      enabled  active    -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:46
msgid ""
"Adding a snap name as an argument will list only those services added by "
"that snap, or with `<snap-name>.<app>`, for a single app:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:48
msgid ""
"$ snap services lxd.daemon    \n"
"Service     Startup  Current  Notes\n"
"lxd.daemon  enabled  active   socket-activated\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:54
msgid ""
"The output includes the service name, whether the service is started when "
"the system starts up, and whether it's currently running."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:56
msgid "Restarting, stopping and starting services"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:58
msgid ""
"Services are restarted using the `snap restart <snap name>` command. This "
"may be necessary if you've made custom changes to the snap application, for "
"example, which the service needs to reload."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:60
msgid ""
"Using both the snap name and the specific app enables you to target a "
"specific service:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:62
msgid ""
"$ snap restart lxd.daemon\n"
"2024-11-21T21:51:06+01:00 INFO Waiting for \"snap.lxd.daemon.service\" to "
"stop.\n"
"Restarted.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:68
msgid ""
"The option to perform an operation on all of a snap's services, or on one "
"specific service, is common to all commands that operate on services."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:70
msgid ""
"If a service supports _reloading_, enabling the service to remain running "
"while loading a new configuration, this can be requested with the `--reload` "
"option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:72
msgid "snap restart --reload lxd.daemon\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:76
msgid ""
"The `start` and `stop` commands control whether a service should be "
"currently running."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:78
msgid ""
"Adding the  `--system` argument can be used to manage system-level services, "
"while `--user` and `--users=all` can be used to manage services for the "
"current user, or for all users, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:80
msgid "The following combination is also valid:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:82
msgid "`snap {start,stop,restart} --system --users=all my-snap`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:84
msgid ""
"As root, the default behavior of not specifying anything is the equivalent "
"of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:86
msgid "`snap {start,stop,restart} --system my-snap`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:88
msgid ""
"If operating on a snap that contains a user-service as root, the switch `--"
"user` or `--users=all` must be provided to indicate that it’s intentional, "
"otherwise an error will be thrown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:90
msgid ""
"To prevent a service from starting on the next boot, use the `--disable` "
"option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:92
msgid "snap stop --disable lxd.daemon\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:96
msgid ""
"The *start* command includes an `--enable` option to re-enable the automatic "
"starting of a service  when the system boots:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:98
msgid "snap start --enable lxd.daemon\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:102
msgid "Disabling and enabling user-services"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:104
msgid ""
"User-services cannot be disabled or enabled individually for users "
"currently, they can only be enabled globally or disabled globally (i.e for "
"all users)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:107
msgid "Inspecting logs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:109
msgid ""
"If you need to see the log output for a snap's services, use the `logs` "
"command. As with the _services_ command, you can specify either a snap name "
"to see the logs for all the services it contains, or the name of a specific "
"service within a snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:114
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap logs lxd\n"
"2018-09-14T10:38:23Z lxd.daemon[11096]: => LXD is ready\n"
"(...)\n"
"\n"
"$ sudo snap logs lxd.daemon\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:04+01:00 lxd.daemon[1389234]: => LXD exited cleanly\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:04+01:00 lxd.daemon[370462]: ==> Stopped LXD\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:04+01:00 lxd.daemon[370462]: => Stopping LXCFS\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:04+01:00 lxd.daemon[555302]: Running destructor lxcfs_exit\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:05+01:00 lxd.daemon[370462]: ==> Stopped LXCFS\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:05+01:00 lxd.daemon[370462]: => Cleaning up PID files\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:05+01:00 lxd.daemon[370462]: => Cleaning up namespaces\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:05+01:00 lxd.daemon[370462]: => All done\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:05+01:00 systemd[1]: snap.lxd.daemon.service: Deactivated "
"successfully.\n"
"2022-07-15T17:13:05+01:00 systemd[1]: Stopped Service for snap application "
"lxd.daemon.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:132
msgid ""
"By default, only the last 10 lines are output. This can be changed with the "
"`-n=` option which can accept either a number or `all` for the entire log:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:134
msgid "snap logs -n=all lxd.daemon\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:138
msgid ""
"Adding the `-f` option will keep log output open so you can follow new "
"entries as they occur:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:140
msgid "sudo snap logs lxd -f\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/control-services.md:144
msgid ""
"The size and rate of log output can be limited by placing a snap within a "
"quota group. See {ref}`Journal log limits <ref-use-resource-quotas_journal-"
"log-limits>` for more details."
msgstr ""
